\section{How the runtime reaches a program}{

One guarded line at the top of each generated program sources \code{autoexec.R}
into the program's own environment, the way a SAS session runs its autoexec
first. Run \verb{Rscript run.R} from the bundle folder to execute programs in
dependency order. For individual programs, first source
\code{source("<bundle>/autoexec.R", chdir = TRUE)}, then source the required
upstream programs before \code{source("<bundle>/programs/adsl.R")}.
\code{autoexec.R} is the bundle's
counterpart of a SAS autoexec: generated by the translation, maintained by
a person afterwards.
Its LIBRARIES section says where each libref reads from and writes to (a
relative path means inside the bundle folder); its LOAD section sources
\code{runtime/sas2r-helpers.R} (this runtime), resolves those paths with
\code{\link[=sas2r_resolve_registry]{sas2r_resolve_registry()}}, and sources \verb{runtime/_sas2r_formats.R} (the compiled
format catalog).

To use the runtime at the console, source the autoexec once --
\code{source("<bundle>/autoexec.R", chdir = TRUE)} -- and attach sas2r when you
want the help pages.
}

